module bindings.c.cursor_loop_missing_end_sentinel;

// A walk that takes a half-open range states its own contract: one pointer to
// read from and one past the last byte it may touch, both in the function's
// signature, and the comparisons that keep the cursor inside it restate the
// same pairing -- `first >= afterLast` names that parameter as this walk's
// end. A loop that dereferences and advances the cursor while relating it to
// nothing anywhere in its own subtree has no bound of its own, so its only
// ways out are the values of the bytes it reads, and a buffer that ends
// mid-walk leaves it reading past the range.
//
// The fact is emitted by the C frontend (ccCursorLoopMissingEndSentinelObservations,
// internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/c.go) from the syntax tree, so neither
// half can be satisfied by prose or by an unrelated identifier. The end
// sentinel is a parameter standing alone as one side of a relational
// comparison whose other side is the bare cursor, which keeps the fact off
// the two idioms that only look like it: an allocation base plus an offset
// (`p < zl + zlbytes` bounds by the base and a length, not by the parameter)
// and a count checked through an out-parameter (`*argidx >= argc` compares a
// dereference, not the cursor). A cursor never compared bare against a
// parameter is not a range walk at all -- the NUL-terminated walk is the
// ordinary safe C idiom exactly when the contract is a terminator rather
// than a range, and it stays unreported.
//
// The loop side is generous in what counts as a bound: any relational
// operator in the loop's own subtree naming the cursor in either operand
// clears the fact, so `in + 1 >= maxbuf` bounds the walk as well as
// `in >= maxbuf` does, and a loop bounded against a local derived from the
// parameter is left alone. An enclosing loop that carries the check
// suppresses the fact for itself, while a nested walk inside it is judged on
// its own subtree.
//
// The issue joins the existing pointer-offset bounds binding rather than a
// new concept, so it carries the same rule: the two provenances ask one
// question -- is the walk's stated end consulted before the cursor is
// advanced past it? -- and differ only in where the code states that end, an
// encoded length inside the stream or a range parameter in the signature.
//
// Residuals, all false-negative: the pairing is intra-function, so a range
// maintained across calls in a helper is invisible; a cursor spelled
// differently at the comparison and the loop does not pair; and a loop whose
// bound is an equality test against a terminator value rather than a
// relational comparison of the cursor is not this fact's subject.
binding cursorLoopMissingEndSentinel {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.kind == "any" and node.analysis == "cursor_loop.missing_end_sentinel"
  emit issue code.PointerOffsetBoundsCheck at node
}
